1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,196
What happens when the price of a

2
00:00:02,196 --> 00:00:04,712
metal doubles in a single morning,

3
00:00:04,712 --> 00:00:07,251
and the market where it trades decides

4
00:00:07,251 --> 00:00:09,367
that none of it happened?

5
00:00:09,817 --> 00:00:16,733
If you like finding the hidden money behind the headlines, hit subscribe and like. It really helps the channel.

6
00:00:22,584 --> 00:00:24,493
In March twenty twenty-two,

7
00:00:24,493 --> 00:00:26,784
it happened in London.

8
00:00:26,824 --> 00:00:28,318
The price of nickel,

9
00:00:28,318 --> 00:00:30,851
a metal used in stainless steel and

10
00:00:30,851 --> 00:00:31,839
batteries,

11
00:00:31,839 --> 00:00:34,118
shot past one hundred and one thousand

12
00:00:34,118 --> 00:00:36,651
three hundred and sixty-five dollars a

13
00:00:36,651 --> 00:00:37,504
tonne.

14
00:00:37,874 --> 00:00:39,932
The London Metal Exchange suspended

15
00:00:39,932 --> 00:00:40,944
trading.

16
00:00:40,984 --> 00:00:43,498
Then it cancelled every trade made that

17
00:00:43,498 --> 00:00:44,160
morning,

18
00:00:44,160 --> 00:00:46,217
worth about thirteen point four billion

19
00:00:46,217 --> 00:00:47,274
dollars.

20
00:00:47,524 --> 00:00:49,654
Some traders lost fortunes they thought

21
00:00:49,654 --> 00:00:50,964
they had made.

22
00:00:51,054 --> 00:00:53,502
Others were saved from ruin.

23
00:00:59,150 --> 00:01:01,179
Who sets the price of the world's

24
00:01:01,179 --> 00:01:02,359
metals?

25
00:01:08,150 --> 00:01:10,367
For most of the metals in your

26
00:01:10,367 --> 00:01:10,894
phone,

27
00:01:10,894 --> 00:01:13,083
your car or your kitchen sink,

28
00:01:13,083 --> 00:01:15,900
a key price is set in London.

29
00:01:16,240 --> 00:01:18,761
The London Metal Exchange trades contracts

30
00:01:18,761 --> 00:01:20,342
to deliver copper,

31
00:01:20,342 --> 00:01:21,465
aluminium,

32
00:01:21,465 --> 00:01:23,276
nickel and other metals,

33
00:01:23,276 --> 00:01:25,480
usually three months ahead.

34
00:01:25,690 --> 00:01:26,513
Miners,

35
00:01:26,513 --> 00:01:29,352
factories and traders use those contracts

36
00:01:29,352 --> 00:01:31,906
to protect themselves against price

37
00:01:31,906 --> 00:01:32,790
swings.

38
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:35,413
And the market runs almost around the

39
00:01:35,413 --> 00:01:35,871
clock,

40
00:01:35,871 --> 00:01:38,526
with seven of its nineteen trading hours

41
00:01:38,526 --> 00:01:41,056
handled overnight from Hong Kong.

42
00:01:41,317 --> 00:01:43,756
Let me draw how a short position

43
00:01:43,756 --> 00:01:44,627
works.

44
00:01:44,797 --> 00:01:47,056
A company that will sell nickel in

45
00:01:47,056 --> 00:01:49,767
the future agrees today to deliver it

46
00:01:49,767 --> 00:01:51,497
at a fixed price.

47
00:01:51,697 --> 00:01:53,187
That is a short.

48
00:01:53,387 --> 00:01:54,949
If the price falls,

49
00:01:54,949 --> 00:01:56,187
it wins.

50
00:01:56,237 --> 00:01:57,750
If the price rises,

51
00:01:57,750 --> 00:01:58,749
it loses,

52
00:01:58,749 --> 00:02:00,800
and the exchange asks it to put

53
00:02:00,800 --> 00:02:03,364
up more cash every day to cover

54
00:02:03,364 --> 00:02:04,477
the loss.

55
00:02:04,737 --> 00:02:07,057
That cash is called margin.

56
00:02:07,207 --> 00:02:09,701
And if the price rises fast enough,

57
00:02:09,701 --> 00:02:11,968
the margin calls can grow faster than

58
00:02:11,968 --> 00:02:13,576
anyone can pay.

59
00:02:13,817 --> 00:02:16,120
In early twenty twenty-two,

60
00:02:16,120 --> 00:02:18,190
there was a very large short in

61
00:02:18,190 --> 00:02:19,307
nickel.

62
00:02:19,337 --> 00:02:21,723
According to the claimants in the later

63
00:02:21,723 --> 00:02:22,352
court case,

64
00:02:22,352 --> 00:02:24,522
it was built up by companies within

65
00:02:24,522 --> 00:02:25,151
Tsingshan,

66
00:02:25,151 --> 00:02:28,137
a Chinese industrial user of nickel.

67
00:02:28,227 --> 00:02:29,795
And much of it was held not

68
00:02:29,795 --> 00:02:31,341
on the exchange itself,

69
00:02:31,341 --> 00:02:33,357
but in private deals between banks and

70
00:02:33,357 --> 00:02:34,007
traders,

71
00:02:34,007 --> 00:02:36,847
called the over the counter market.

72
00:02:36,887 --> 00:02:39,642
The exchange could not see it.

73
00:02:39,884 --> 00:02:40,371
Then,

74
00:02:40,371 --> 00:02:42,398
at the end of February,

75
00:02:42,398 --> 00:02:44,794
Russia invaded Ukraine.

76
00:02:44,854 --> 00:02:46,952
And as one of the exchange's own

77
00:02:46,952 --> 00:02:48,328
board members would note,

78
00:02:48,328 --> 00:02:50,892
Russia is a large global producer of

79
00:02:50,892 --> 00:02:51,959
nickel.

80
00:02:57,617 --> 00:03:00,521
What happens when everyone who bet on

81
00:03:00,521 --> 00:03:03,154
a fall has to buy at once?

82
00:03:06,617 --> 00:03:09,947
The pressure built over a few days.

83
00:03:09,947 --> 00:03:10,856
On Friday,

84
00:03:10,856 --> 00:03:11,532
March fourth,

85
00:03:11,532 --> 00:03:13,862
the exchange's clearing house asked its

86
00:03:13,862 --> 00:03:15,960
members for a record three point five

87
00:03:15,960 --> 00:03:18,327
billion dollars in extra margin.

88
00:03:18,327 --> 00:03:19,821
On Monday morning,

89
00:03:19,821 --> 00:03:22,353
it asked for another five point one

90
00:03:22,353 --> 00:03:23,594
billion dollars,

91
00:03:23,594 --> 00:03:25,017
a new record.

92
00:03:25,017 --> 00:03:26,838
By the end of Monday,

93
00:03:26,838 --> 00:03:30,004
nickel had jumped by sixty-six percent in

94
00:03:30,004 --> 00:03:31,297
a single day,

95
00:03:31,297 --> 00:03:33,936
to forty-eight thousand and seventy-five

96
00:03:33,936 --> 00:03:35,592
dollars a tonne.

97
00:03:35,850 --> 00:03:37,268
Inside the exchange,

98
00:03:37,268 --> 00:03:39,220
people were alarmed.

99
00:03:39,220 --> 00:03:41,694
By two in the afternoon on Monday,

100
00:03:41,694 --> 00:03:43,737
the clearing house had called more than

101
00:03:43,737 --> 00:03:46,008
seven point four billion dollars from its

102
00:03:46,008 --> 00:03:47,120
members that day,

103
00:03:47,120 --> 00:03:49,617
and one manager warned in an email

104
00:03:49,617 --> 00:03:51,433
that the calls were adding to the

105
00:03:51,433 --> 00:03:52,260
stress.

106
00:03:52,500 --> 00:03:54,283
A board member wrote that they did

107
00:03:54,283 --> 00:03:56,958
not understand why nickel was up sixty-six

108
00:03:56,958 --> 00:03:59,610
percent while aluminium was down three,

109
00:03:59,610 --> 00:04:01,770
when Russia produced both.

110
00:04:01,850 --> 00:04:04,091
Another described the metals markets to

111
00:04:04,091 --> 00:04:07,119
the British Treasury as in total disarray.

112
00:04:07,384 --> 00:04:09,434
Then came the night.

113
00:04:09,464 --> 00:04:12,300
Trading reopened in the early hours of

114
00:04:12,300 --> 00:04:13,047
Tuesday,

115
00:04:13,047 --> 00:04:14,053
London time,

116
00:04:14,053 --> 00:04:15,684
run from Hong Kong.

117
00:04:15,734 --> 00:04:18,519
The exchange had automatic limits,

118
00:04:18,519 --> 00:04:19,770
called price bands,

119
00:04:19,770 --> 00:04:22,414
to stop prices moving too fast.

120
00:04:22,794 --> 00:04:25,029
But at four forty-nine in the morning,

121
00:04:25,029 --> 00:04:26,654
they were switched off.

122
00:04:26,714 --> 00:04:28,368
For more than three hours,

123
00:04:28,368 --> 00:04:31,771
there was no automatic brake at all.

124
00:04:32,017 --> 00:04:33,648
Without the brake,

125
00:04:33,648 --> 00:04:35,247
the price ran.

126
00:04:35,277 --> 00:04:37,673
From sixty thousand to seventy thousand

127
00:04:37,673 --> 00:04:39,797
dollars in under an hour.

128
00:04:39,807 --> 00:04:40,197
Then,

129
00:04:40,197 --> 00:04:42,021
in just twenty-four minutes,

130
00:04:42,021 --> 00:04:44,277
from seventy thousand to one hundred and

131
00:04:44,277 --> 00:04:46,327
one thousand three hundred and sixty-five

132
00:04:46,327 --> 00:04:47,337
dollars.

133
00:04:47,487 --> 00:04:49,505
The price had doubled since the close

134
00:04:49,505 --> 00:04:51,367
of the day before.

135
00:04:51,567 --> 00:04:52,562
In London,

136
00:04:52,562 --> 00:04:54,857
a board member of the exchange woke

137
00:04:54,857 --> 00:04:56,617
up around half past five,

138
00:04:56,617 --> 00:04:58,122
checked the prices,

139
00:04:58,122 --> 00:04:59,997
and saw the numbers.

140
00:05:00,250 --> 00:05:02,499
Let me draw the morning of March

141
00:05:02,499 --> 00:05:03,380
eighth.

142
00:05:03,620 --> 00:05:05,800
Here is the price on Friday.

143
00:05:05,930 --> 00:05:07,426
Here is Monday's close,

144
00:05:07,426 --> 00:05:09,800
sixty-six percent higher.

145
00:05:10,040 --> 00:05:11,892
And here is the early hours of

146
00:05:11,892 --> 00:05:12,563
Tuesday,

147
00:05:12,563 --> 00:05:14,877
climbing to more than one hundred thousand

148
00:05:14,877 --> 00:05:15,940
dollars.

149
00:05:16,350 --> 00:05:18,640
Now look at the bars underneath.

150
00:05:18,680 --> 00:05:20,270
The margin calls.

151
00:05:20,270 --> 00:05:22,480
Three and a half billion on Friday.

152
00:05:22,480 --> 00:05:24,330
Five billion on Monday.

153
00:05:24,330 --> 00:05:25,986
And on Tuesday morning,

154
00:05:25,986 --> 00:05:28,026
almost twenty billion.

155
00:05:28,284 --> 00:05:30,872
At seven thirty in the morning,

156
00:05:30,872 --> 00:05:33,564
the exchange's managers met.

157
00:05:33,814 --> 00:05:35,796
They had less than an hour to

158
00:05:35,796 --> 00:05:36,962
decide.

159
00:05:42,617 --> 00:05:44,980
Can a market just decide that trades

160
00:05:44,980 --> 00:05:46,762
never happened?

161
00:05:51,617 --> 00:05:54,667
The numbers on the table were frightening.

162
00:05:54,667 --> 00:05:56,659
That morning's margin calls on nickel

163
00:05:56,659 --> 00:05:59,094
totalled nineteen point seven billion

164
00:05:59,094 --> 00:06:00,097
dollars.

165
00:06:00,097 --> 00:06:02,048
According to the regulator,

166
00:06:02,048 --> 00:06:04,018
calls on that scale were likely to

167
00:06:04,018 --> 00:06:06,383
push several members of the exchange into

168
00:06:06,383 --> 00:06:07,377
default.

169
00:06:07,587 --> 00:06:09,572
And when a member defaults,

170
00:06:09,572 --> 00:06:11,834
the losses can spread through the clearing

171
00:06:11,834 --> 00:06:13,942
house to everyone else.

172
00:06:14,184 --> 00:06:15,424
At eight fifteen,

173
00:06:15,424 --> 00:06:18,554
the exchange suspended nickel trading.

174
00:06:18,634 --> 00:06:19,636
Later that day,

175
00:06:19,636 --> 00:06:21,054
it went further.

176
00:06:21,094 --> 00:06:23,990
It cancelled every nickel trade made since

177
00:06:23,990 --> 00:06:24,690
midnight,

178
00:06:24,690 --> 00:06:26,861
worth about thirteen point four billion

179
00:06:26,861 --> 00:06:27,944
dollars.

180
00:06:28,224 --> 00:06:30,129
The market stayed shut for more than

181
00:06:30,129 --> 00:06:30,819
a week,

182
00:06:30,819 --> 00:06:33,134
and reopened on March sixteenth.

183
00:06:33,134 --> 00:06:35,240
Night trading from Asia did not return

184
00:06:35,240 --> 00:06:36,893
for a whole year.

185
00:06:37,150 --> 00:06:39,228
The exchange chose midnight as the

186
00:06:39,228 --> 00:06:39,982
cut-off,

187
00:06:39,982 --> 00:06:42,920
not the moment the brakes came off.

188
00:06:43,160 --> 00:06:45,340
Everything traded from the start of the

189
00:06:45,340 --> 00:06:46,510
day was wiped out,

190
00:06:46,510 --> 00:06:49,290
as if the morning had never happened.

191
00:06:49,530 --> 00:06:51,622
And the billions in margin that the

192
00:06:51,622 --> 00:06:53,714
morning's prices had created simply

193
00:06:53,714 --> 00:06:55,709
disappeared with it.

194
00:06:55,950 --> 00:06:58,360
Think about what that meant.

195
00:06:58,360 --> 00:07:00,557
A trader who had sold nickel at

196
00:07:00,557 --> 00:07:02,997
one hundred thousand dollars that morning

197
00:07:02,997 --> 00:07:05,550
suddenly had no trade at all.

198
00:07:05,730 --> 00:07:07,584
And a company caught short,

199
00:07:07,584 --> 00:07:09,439
facing billions in losses,

200
00:07:09,439 --> 00:07:12,021
was suddenly spared the worst prices of

201
00:07:12,021 --> 00:07:13,090
the day.

202
00:07:13,300 --> 00:07:15,932
The cancellation saved the market from a

203
00:07:15,932 --> 00:07:17,410
chain of defaults.

204
00:07:17,520 --> 00:07:19,693
But it also took real profits away

205
00:07:19,693 --> 00:07:22,249
from people who had done nothing wrong.

206
00:07:22,484 --> 00:07:25,706
Some of those people went to court.

207
00:07:31,350 --> 00:07:34,819
Was it legal to erase the trades?

208
00:07:40,350 --> 00:07:42,673
The American hedge fund Elliott said the

209
00:07:42,673 --> 00:07:44,785
cancellation had cost it about four

210
00:07:44,785 --> 00:07:47,108
hundred and fifty-six million dollars in

211
00:07:47,108 --> 00:07:48,130
profits.

212
00:07:48,430 --> 00:07:50,381
The trading firm Jane Street put its

213
00:07:50,381 --> 00:07:53,420
loss at about fifteen million dollars.

214
00:07:53,840 --> 00:07:55,857
They argued that the market had not

215
00:07:55,857 --> 00:07:57,628
been disorderly at all,

216
00:07:57,628 --> 00:07:59,646
that the price was simply the result

217
00:07:59,646 --> 00:08:01,865
of a squeeze on traders like Tsingshan,

218
00:08:01,865 --> 00:08:03,658
and that the exchange had acted

219
00:08:03,658 --> 00:08:05,155
unlawfully.

220
00:08:05,417 --> 00:08:08,053
In November twenty twenty-three,

221
00:08:08,053 --> 00:08:10,689
the High Court in London disagreed,

222
00:08:10,689 --> 00:08:12,887
and dismissed the case.

223
00:08:13,077 --> 00:08:15,030
The traders had signed up to the

224
00:08:15,030 --> 00:08:16,310
exchange's rules,

225
00:08:16,310 --> 00:08:18,262
and those rules gave it the power

226
00:08:18,262 --> 00:08:19,947
to cancel trades.

227
00:08:20,177 --> 00:08:22,375
In October twenty twenty-four,

228
00:08:22,375 --> 00:08:25,417
the Court of Appeal upheld that decision.

229
00:08:25,557 --> 00:08:27,020
The cancellation,

230
00:08:27,020 --> 00:08:27,987
the courts found,

231
00:08:27,987 --> 00:08:29,579
had been lawful.

232
00:08:29,817 --> 00:08:32,059
Let me draw the dilemma the exchange

233
00:08:32,059 --> 00:08:33,107
faced.

234
00:08:33,207 --> 00:08:34,351
Option one,

235
00:08:34,351 --> 00:08:36,417
let the trades stand.

236
00:08:36,497 --> 00:08:38,477
Then several members might default,

237
00:08:38,477 --> 00:08:40,077
and the damage could spread through the

238
00:08:40,077 --> 00:08:41,877
whole clearing system.

239
00:08:42,127 --> 00:08:43,249
Option two,

240
00:08:43,249 --> 00:08:45,287
cancel the trades.

241
00:08:45,297 --> 00:08:46,826
The system survives,

242
00:08:46,826 --> 00:08:48,821
but some traders lose money that they

243
00:08:48,821 --> 00:08:50,307
had fairly won.

244
00:08:50,597 --> 00:08:52,495
The arrow from each option leads to

245
00:08:52,495 --> 00:08:53,727
a loser.

246
00:08:53,847 --> 00:08:55,871
The exchange chose the loser it thought

247
00:08:55,871 --> 00:08:57,821
the market could survive.

248
00:08:58,084 --> 00:09:01,214
The courts said the decision was lawful.

249
00:09:01,324 --> 00:09:04,144
The regulator asked a different question.

250
00:09:04,194 --> 00:09:06,361
How did it get that far?

251
00:09:12,017 --> 00:09:14,955
Who was responsible for the chaos?

252
00:09:21,017 --> 00:09:22,810
In March twenty twenty-five,

253
00:09:22,810 --> 00:09:25,306
Britain's Financial Conduct Authority

254
00:09:25,306 --> 00:09:27,802
fined the London Metal Exchange about

255
00:09:27,802 --> 00:09:29,337
twelve million dollars.

256
00:09:29,337 --> 00:09:31,698
It found that the exchange's policies,

257
00:09:31,698 --> 00:09:34,080
controls and training had not prepared it

258
00:09:34,080 --> 00:09:36,413
for extreme volatility.

259
00:09:36,650 --> 00:09:39,531
The regulator also described the culture

260
00:09:39,531 --> 00:09:41,460
behind the decisions.

261
00:09:41,660 --> 00:09:44,146
The prevailing philosophy at the exchange,

262
00:09:44,146 --> 00:09:44,705
it said,

263
00:09:44,705 --> 00:09:46,632
was that trading should not be restricted

264
00:09:46,632 --> 00:09:49,310
even in times of extreme volatility,

265
00:09:49,310 --> 00:09:52,030
unless the market became disorderly.

266
00:09:52,200 --> 00:09:54,460
Its price bands had been designed mainly

267
00:09:54,460 --> 00:09:55,794
to catch mistakes,

268
00:09:55,794 --> 00:09:57,828
a fat finger on a keyboard or

269
00:09:57,828 --> 00:09:59,990
a rogue computer program.

270
00:09:59,990 --> 00:10:01,136
Not a real squeeze,

271
00:10:01,136 --> 00:10:02,984
in the middle of the night,

272
00:10:02,984 --> 00:10:04,754
with billions at stake.

273
00:10:05,017 --> 00:10:07,393
The notice describes an exchange that

274
00:10:07,393 --> 00:10:09,657
could not see the risk building.

275
00:10:09,667 --> 00:10:11,547
Its overnight team in Hong Kong had

276
00:10:11,547 --> 00:10:13,218
switched off the price bands without

277
00:10:13,218 --> 00:10:15,285
consulting senior management,

278
00:10:15,285 --> 00:10:17,583
and had never been trained to escalate

279
00:10:17,583 --> 00:10:19,227
such a decision.

280
00:10:19,637 --> 00:10:21,371
And it could not see the private

281
00:10:21,371 --> 00:10:22,433
positions that,

282
00:10:22,433 --> 00:10:23,495
it later found,

283
00:10:23,495 --> 00:10:25,445
were a material cause of the price

284
00:10:25,445 --> 00:10:26,262
rise.

285
00:10:26,517 --> 00:10:29,175
So what does the nickel crisis teach

286
00:10:29,175 --> 00:10:30,957
about markets?

287
00:10:31,177 --> 00:10:33,038
That a price is only as real

288
00:10:33,038 --> 00:10:35,267
as the system behind it.

289
00:10:35,497 --> 00:10:37,666
When enough people bet one way,

290
00:10:37,666 --> 00:10:39,834
and the bets are hidden from view,

291
00:10:39,834 --> 00:10:41,783
a market can break in a few

292
00:10:41,783 --> 00:10:42,627
hours.

293
